<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">
Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jonas Bergström and Thomas Wennink
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:jonasb@math.su.se">jonasb@math.su.se</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:thomas.wennink@math.su.se">thomas.wennink@math.su.se</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We have written a computer program that implements Deligne’s pullback and pushforward weight spectral sequences to compute the weight graded pieces of the rational cohomology of moduli spaces of pointed smooth curves (as well as curves of compact type and curves with rational tails) in cases where the cohomology groups appearing in the boundary stratification of the Deligne-Mumford compactification are generated by tautological classes (and when Pixton’s relations are all relations).
The weight graded pieces are computed together with the induced action of the symmetric group permuting the points on the curves.
Using the computer program we have determined this information in the case of genus five as well as in the case of genus three with three marked points.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the moduli spaces of smooth, respectively stable, <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-pointed curves of genus <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. In this article we implement Deligne’s pullback and pushforward weight spectral sequences to compute weight graded pieces of the compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in cases where the <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page, which is built out of pieces of cohomology groups of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and of strata in the boundary <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\setminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\setminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, consists only of tautological cohomology. Using this implementation we proved Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> below.
In the whole of the article, cohomology will mean either <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic étale cohomology or of rational Hodge structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The symmetric group <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it induces an action on the cohomology groups of these spaces.
This action also respects the weight filtration <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (induced by Deligne’s weight spectral sequences).
To each partition <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> we associate, in the usual way, an irreducible representation <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
What we aim to compute is then, for each <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q,r</annotation></semantics></math>, the following decomposition of the weight graded pieces,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{gr}_{q}H^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=W_{q}H^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/W_{q-1}H^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}\left(U^{q,r,\lambda}_{g,n}\boxtimes s_{\lambda}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊠</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{gr}_{q}H^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=W_{q}H^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/W_{q-1}H^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}\left(U^{q,r,\lambda}_{g,n}\boxtimes s_{\lambda}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for some (pure) rational Hodge structures (respectively <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations) <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{q,r,\lambda}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{q,r,\lambda}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Poincaré duality we then get that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{gr}_{q}H_{c}^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\mathrm{gr}_{2(3g-3+n)-q}H^{2(3g-3+n)-r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{gr}_{q}H_{c}^{r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\mathrm{gr}_{2(3g-3+n)-q}H^{2(3g-3+n)-r}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general, we know that for <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;2g</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;2g+1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;2g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;2g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite>. The results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> have made the weight <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> part of the compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> amenable to computation.
Furthermore, the weight graded pieces for odd weights up to <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> all vanish, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.
Finally, the isomorphism in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Proposition 1.7]</cite> gives a way to compute the weight <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> contribution to any compactly supported cohomology group of <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The weight graded pieces (or indeed the Betti numbers) of the cohomology of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> have only been fully determined for a few <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For results in <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, in <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, in <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>, in <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> and finally for <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Theorem 1.2]</cite>. As far as we know, there is for <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> a lack of published results. Combining the methods in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, Louis Hainaut has computed all results for <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math> (personal communication). All these results (except for the case of <math id="S1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>), have been verified using our implementation and are available at <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite>, if <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3g+2n&lt;25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3g+2n&lt;25</annotation></semantics></math> (a range that covers all results mentioned above as well as in the following two theorems), the cohomology will be of Tate type. This means that the odd weight graded pieces vanish and that a <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2q</annotation></semantics></math>-weight graded piece of a cohomology group will be a direct sum of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>th tensor products of the Tate class <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}=H^{2}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕃</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}=H^{2}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. The associated pure rational Hodge structure of <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> has Hodge weight <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and the associated <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representation is the cyclotomic character.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the following two theorems the weight filtration of each cohomology group consists of only one step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Tate type and equals,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=1,\quad H^{2}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L},\quad H^{4}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{2},\quad H^{5}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{3},\\
H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{3},\quad H^{7}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{4},\quad H^{14}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>14</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>12</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=1,\quad H^{2}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L},\quad H^{4}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{2},\quad H^{5}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{3},\\
H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{3},\quad H^{7}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{4},\quad H^{14}(\mathcal{M}_{5})=\mathbb{L}^{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rational Chow ring of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1}]/\kappa_{1}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1}]/\kappa_{1}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. The cycle class map from the rational Chow ring into rational cohomology is in this case injective, giving the contributions <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in degrees <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The moduli space <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> can be stratified into the hyperelliptic locus <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, the trigonal locus <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and an affine open locus of general curves <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. The rational cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> equals that of a point. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, the rational cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> was determined. Orsola Tommasi and Angelina Zheng then conjectured (building upon earlier computations of Orsola Tommasi using the Gorinov-Vassiliev method applied to <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>), that the rational cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> would consist of a class <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. Another way to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> would be to prove this conjecture about the rational cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Tate type and equals,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=s_{3},\quad H^{2}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=(2s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L},\quad H^{4}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=(s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L}^{2},\\
H^{5}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=2(s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L}^{3},\quad H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=s_{3}\mathbb{L}^{6},\quad H^{8}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=2(s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L}^{7}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>8</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=s_{3},\quad H^{2}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=(2s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L},\quad H^{4}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=(s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L}^{2},\\
H^{5}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=2(s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L}^{3},\quad H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=s_{3}\mathbb{L}^{6},\quad H^{8}(\mathcal{M}_{3,3})=2(s_{3}+s_{2,1})\mathbb{L}^{7}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Euler characteristic of <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Grothendieck group of mixed rational Hodge structures (or <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations) had already been computed and will appear in forthcoming work by the first author together with Samir Canning, Dan Petersen and Johannes Schmitt.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our program builds upon the SageMath module <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and improves parts of it. Our code will be available at <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> and we plan to integrate it into the <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> package.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using <span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> (and checking that Pixton’s relations are all relations, see further in section <a href="#S4" title="4. Tautological cohomology ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5" title="5. The computer implementation ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), one can show that in degrees <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Whenever we compute the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we also compute the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{rt}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{rt}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space of curves of compact type, respectively with rational tails.
This is included in our list of results at <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
The idea to apply Deligne’s weight spectral sequence to the moduli space of curves of compact type comes from forthcoming work by Amy Bradford.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are most grateful to Amy Bradford, Samir Canning, Hannah Larson, Sam Payne, Dan Petersen, Johannes Schmitt, Orsola Tommasi, Thomas Willwacher and Angelina Zheng for valuable discussions and comments, and to Samir Canning for suggesting to implement Deligne’s weight spectral sequences.
During this work, the second author was supported by a Sverker Lerheden fellowship for postdoctoral studies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Deligne’s pushforward weight spectral sequence</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Section 7]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Section 2.3]</cite>, but write the differential in a slightly more explicit way which better corresponds to our concrete computations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To an <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-pointed genus <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> stable graph <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting its set of vertices respectively edges, we associate a product of moduli spaces and a gluing map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\prod_{v\in V_{\Gamma}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g(v),n(v)},\qquad\xi_{\Gamma}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\prod_{v\in V_{\Gamma}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g(v),n(v)},\qquad\xi_{\Gamma}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For any stable graph <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we have an action of <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, inducing an action on <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Deligne’s pushforward weight spectral sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Théorème (3.2.5)]</cite> gives, in the case of <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the form,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{-p,q}=\bigoplus_{|E_{\Gamma}|=p}(H^{q-2p}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}))^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{p}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{-p,q}=\bigoplus_{|E_{\Gamma}|=p}(H^{q-2p}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}))^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> by permutations.
This spectral sequence degenerates at the second page and abuts to the weight <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> graded piece,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}^{-p,q}=\mathrm{gr}_{q}H^{q-p}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}^{-p,q}=\mathrm{gr}_{q}H^{q-p}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For every isomorphism class of stable graphs we choose a representative <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and we also fix an ordering of its edges <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\Gamma}:E_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\{1,\ldots,|E_{\Gamma}|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\Gamma}:E_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\{1,\ldots,|E_{\Gamma}|\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the stable graph <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the graph obtained by contracting its <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th edge.
It inherits an ordering <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\Gamma_{i}}:E_{\Gamma_{i}}\rightarrow\{1,\ldots,i-1,i+1,\ldots,|E_{\Gamma}|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\Gamma_{i}}:E_{\Gamma_{i}}\rightarrow\{1,\ldots,i-1,i+1,\ldots,|E_{\Gamma}|\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the chosen representative of the isomorphism class of <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix an isomorphism <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\Gamma_{i}\rightarrow\Gamma^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\Gamma_{i}\rightarrow\Gamma^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each of the edge contractions we have a gluing map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\xrightarrow{\xi_{\Gamma,\Gamma_{i}}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{i}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ξ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\xrightarrow{\xi_{\Gamma,\Gamma_{i}}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and, for each <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we get the differential restricted to <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{1}:\bigl(H^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\rm{Aut}(\Gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{1}:\bigl(H^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\rm{Aut}(\Gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rightarrow\bigoplus_{|E_{\Gamma^{\prime}}|=|E_{\Gamma}|-1}\bigl(H^{r+2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma^{\prime}})\bigr)^{\rm{Aut}(\Gamma^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rightarrow\bigoplus_{|E_{\Gamma^{\prime}}|=|E_{\Gamma}|-1}\bigl(H^{r+2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma^{\prime}})\bigr)^{\rm{Aut}(\Gamma^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto\sum_{i=1}^{|E_{\Gamma}|}\frac{1}{|\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma^{\prime}_{i})|}\sum_{\psi\in\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma^{\prime}_{i})}\textrm{sgn}(\sigma_{i,\psi})\cdot(\psi\circ\phi\circ\xi_{\Gamma,\Gamma_{i}})_{*}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto\sum_{i=1}^{|E_{\Gamma}|}\frac{1}{|\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma^{\prime}_{i})|}\sum_{\psi\in\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma^{\prime}_{i})}\textrm{sgn}(\sigma_{i,\psi})\cdot(\psi\circ\phi\circ\xi_{\Gamma,\Gamma_{i}})_{*}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,\psi}:=\alpha_{\Gamma^{\prime}_{i}}\circ\psi_{E}\circ\phi_{E}\circ\beta^{-1}_{\Gamma_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,\psi}:=\alpha_{\Gamma^{\prime}_{i}}\circ\psi_{E}\circ\phi_{E}\circ\beta^{-1}_{\Gamma_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation of <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,|E_{\Gamma}|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,|E_{\Gamma}|\}</annotation></semantics></math> and where <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{E},\phi_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{E},\phi_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the induced maps on edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the following stable graphs with ordered edges</p>
