><semantics><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 35.63 0 C 35.63 3.37 32.9 6.1 29.53 6.1 C 26.16 6.1 23.43 3.37 23.43 0 C 23.43 -3.37 26.16 -6.1 29.53 -6.1 C 32.9 -6.1 35.63 -3.37 35.63 0 Z M 29.53 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 26.77 -3.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.24)" width="5.52"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -23.15 0 C -16.61 0 -12.92 0 -6.38 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -18.66 4.08)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:7.36pt;" height="4.02" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 2.98)" width="7.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">e</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.38 0 C 12.92 0 16.61 0 23.15 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.87 4.08)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:7.36pt;" height="4.02" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 2.98)" width="7.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">e</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -31.18 6.16 C -33.84 16.11 -25.21 16.11 -27.88 6.16"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.43 17.7)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:7.36pt;" height="4.02" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 2.98)" width="7.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">e</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Compact type and rational tails</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Just like in the pushforward case, we can calculate Deligne’s pullback spectral sequence for moduli spaces of curves of compact type (resp. with rational tails) by restricting the graphs we sum over.
Unlike in the pushforward case, we now also need to restrict the differential map.
This is done by not allowing the splitting of a vertex in a way that would result in a graph with a bridge (resp. rational tail).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Tautological cohomology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our computations will be done using tautological cohomology, which we briefly describe here.
For any stable graph <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and monomial <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes pulled back from the projection onto the factors of <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a class in the Chow ring <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> called a decorated stratum class.
Let <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g,n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g,n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space with the decorated stratum classes as a basis. To any decorated stratum, corresponding to <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma,\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, we get an element,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{|\text{Aut}(\Gamma)|}{\xi_{\Gamma}}_{*}(\theta)\in A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{|\text{Aut}(\Gamma)|}{\xi_{\Gamma}}_{*}(\theta)\in A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The image of <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g,n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g,n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological ring <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
See for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The image of <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> in cohomology <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{2*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, under the cycle class map, will be denoted by <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and be called the tautological cohomology. If <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=H^{2r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=H^{2r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> then we will say that the cohomology (group) is tautological.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, a set <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{g,n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{g,n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of relations among the generators <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g,n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g,n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> mapped inside <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, called Pixton’s relations (and also called the generalized Faber-Zagier relations), were given and were conjectured to be all relations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>The computer implementation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have implemented Deligne’s pushforward and pullback weight spectral sequences described in sections <a href="#S2" title="2. Deligne’s pushforward weight spectral sequence ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3" title="3. Deligne’s pullback weight spectral sequence ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> into a program that takes any pair <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> and computes (under the assumption that Pixton’s relations are all relations, see below) the weight graded pieces of the rational cohomology of <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The implementation is based upon knowledge of the tautological cohomology of moduli spaces of stable pointed curves and builds upon the SageMath module <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since our implementation is based upon computations in the tautological cohomology ring, we need to make sure that the <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the spectral sequences consists of tautological cohomology. But if <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\tilde{g}+\tilde{n}\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\tilde{g}+\tilde{n}\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> then by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> the cohomology of <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\tilde{g},\tilde{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\tilde{g},\tilde{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological, and hence for all <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n,p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n,p,q</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{-p+i,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{-p+i,q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}_{1}^{p+i,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}_{1}^{p+i,q}</annotation></semantics></math> will be tautological. Note that since tautological cohomology is of Tate type, it follows that the cohomology of <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> will be of Tate type. Note also that the assumption that all of the cohomology of <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological implies that the tautological cohomology is Gorenstein, because of Poincaré duality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will use Pixton’s relations to describe the relations between the generators of the tautological cohomology, and hence we need to show that these are indeed all relations for the tautological cohomology groups contributing to the <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the spectral sequences. This condition has been checked in all cases (sometimes by checking the Poincaré dual cohomology group) appearing in the computations to prove Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. This check was based upon the fact that if <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological and <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{2r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\dim(S_{g,n}^{r}/P_{g,n}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{2r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\dim(S_{g,n}^{r}/P_{g,n}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>, then Pixton’s relations are all relations. All the Betti numbers necessary for the check (for Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) were already known except the ones for <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. The computation of the Betti numbers of <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> will be part of forthcoming work by the first author together with Samir Canning, Dan Petersen and Johannes Schmitt.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>The algorithm</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The general outline of our algorithm is as follows. Note that this does not include the refinements discussed in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Computing the tautological ring of the moduli space of stable curves ‣ 5. The computer implementation ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As input to the algorithm, we decide which weights we compute using the pushforward spectral sequence and for which weights we instead use the pullback spectral sequence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Then, for all <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\vdash n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\vdash n</annotation></semantics></math> and all relevant graphs <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute a basis for each tautological cohomology group <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}^{r}/P^{r}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}^{r}/P^{r}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that appears in the terms of <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bigl(\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\Bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigl(\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\Bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also keep track of how to map any element of <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}^{r^{\prime}})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}^{r^{\prime}})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to this basis.
Here <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}:=\prod\mathbb{S}_{\lambda_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}:=\prod\mathbb{S}_{\lambda_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subgroup of <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, permuting the marked points.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> also acts on the graphs appearing in the terms of the spectral sequences.
We choose a representative <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for each orbit.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Then for each such graph <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> we compute a basis for each <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bigl(\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\Bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigl(\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\Bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> that appears in the spectral sequence.
This includes keeping track of how elements of <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> map to this basis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case where the <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is trivial (i.e. <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=[1,\ldots,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=[1,\ldots,1]</annotation></semantics></math>), we now compute the image of the differential.
For all other <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we symmetrize this image. That is, we send every element of the image to a fixed representative of its <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the ranks of the images and of the <math id="S5.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bigl(\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\Bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigl(\bigl(H^{q}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\Bigr)^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the ranks of the groups on the second page of the spectral sequence up to <math id="S5.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We express the <math id="S5.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the irreducible representations of <math id="S5.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The order in which we do the steps is not exactly as described above.
In particular, we first do all computations for <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=[1,\ldots,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=[1,\ldots,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, where the action is trivial.
Then, for other <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we can restrict our computations to those that contribute to parts of the second page that are nonzero for the trivial action.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During the computations we save partial results, such as the image of the page one differential restricted to a specific graph.
Thus, computing the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{rt}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{rt}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> simply becomes a matter of combining these partial results in a different manner.
Note that this might require us to now compute partial results that we previously skipped due to Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Remark 5.1. ‣ 5.1. The algorithm ‣ 5. The computer implementation ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The SageMath module <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> could already compute the generators, relations, and bases for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
But we added some optimizations to the original base computation code in order to reach the cases we need.
Furthermore, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> can compute pushforwards and pullbacks of elements of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> under gluing maps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Computing the tautological ring of the moduli space of stable curves</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main bottleneck in our computations is that of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in the factors of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We need a basis of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and a way to convert any decorated stratum class to this basis.
The numbers of generators and relations grow quickly with <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n,r</annotation></semantics></math>, and so solving the system of equations demands large amounts of memory already for small values of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n,r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the number of relations obtained from Pixton’s formula grows similarly to the number of partitions of size at most <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3r-g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3r-g-1</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>).
Using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Proposition 2]</cite> one can alternatively compute the relations using the recursive structure of the boundary.
This was originally implemented by Pixton for the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part of the cohomology.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> it was optimized and extended to the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part of the cohomology for <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n^{\prime}\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n^{\prime}\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.
It provides a significant improvement over directly using Pixton’s formula except for very small <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
However it has not been implemented for general <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> invariants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another way to deal with the large number of relations is that, if <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r&gt;3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r&gt;3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can use the pairing in cases that it is perfect (which holds if <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>), to compute <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{3g-3+n-r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{3g-3+n-r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This requires computing the multiplication matrix for multiplying the elements of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g,n}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g,n}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> with a basis of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{3g-3+n-r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{3g-3+n-r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of elements in this basis is a lot smaller than the number of Pixton’s relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In some cases we can use the symmetry of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to lower the number of decorated stratum classes we need to consider.
Let <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\subset\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\subset\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> be the subgroup generated by the automorphisms that swap the two half-edges of a self-edge of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and the automorphisms that swap two self-edges of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define</p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{r}(\Gamma,v)=(S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)}\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}(v,v)))^{G_{v}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{r}(\Gamma,v)=(S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)}\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}(v,v)))^{G_{v}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\Gamma,v)=H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g(v),n(v)}\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}(v,v)))^{G_{v}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\Gamma,v)=H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g(v),n(v)}\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}(v,v)))^{G_{v}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\Gamma}(v,v)\subset E_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\Gamma}(v,v)\subset E_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of self-edges at <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because</p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma},\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}))^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\cong\Bigl(\bigl(\prod_{v\in{V_{\Gamma}}}H^{*}(\Gamma,v)\bigr)\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\Bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)/(\bigoplus_{v}G_{v})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">⨁</mo><mi>v</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma},\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma}))^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)}\cong\Bigl(\bigl(\prod_{v\in{V_{\Gamma}}}H^{*}(\Gamma,v)\bigr)\otimes\mathrm{det}(E_{\Gamma})\Bigr)^{\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)/(\bigoplus_{v}G_{v})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">we can compute Pixton’s relations on <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{r}(\Gamma,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{r}(\Gamma,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of self-edges at <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the subgroup <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}\subset G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}\subset G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> that permutes the half-edges of each self-edge.
Let <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in RH^{3g-3+n-r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in RH^{3g-3+n-r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>,
then</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sum_{g\in\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}}g(y)=\sum_{g\in\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}}g(xy)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sum_{g\in\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}}g(y)=\sum_{g\in\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}}g(xy).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">So when we use the perfect pairing, we can calculate the multiplication matrix for multiplying with <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{r}_{g(v),n(v)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unfortunately the same does not hold for the action by the whole of <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we cannot use it in combination with the perfect pairing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph consisting of a single vertex of genus <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5-g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5-g</annotation></semantics></math> self-edges.
Here is some data that illustrates how the number of decorated stratum classes grows.</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
