<th id="S5.Ex19.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(S^{3}_{g,10-2g})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{5-g}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(S^{3}_{g,10-2g})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{5-g}}|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65380" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>65380</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65380</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29749" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29749</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29749</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8398" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8398</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8398</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1974" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1974</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1974</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="456" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>456</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">456</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="109" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>109</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">109</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.Ex19.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.Ex19.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(S^{4}_{g,10-2g})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{5-g}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(S^{4}_{g,10-2g})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{5-g}}|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="651710" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>651710</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">651710</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="270478" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>270478</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">270478</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66650" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66650</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66650</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13475" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13475</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13475</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2581" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2581</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2581</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.6.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="518" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>518</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">518</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.Ex19.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.Ex19.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S^{4}(\Gamma_{g},1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S^{4}(\Gamma_{g},1)|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2677" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2677</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2677</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6759" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6759</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6759</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8151" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8151</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8151</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5943" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5943</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5943</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2581" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2581</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2581</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.Ex19.1.7.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="518" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>518</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">518</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that the actions by <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}^{5-g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}^{5-g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> provide a significant improvement.
Because of this, computing the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{4,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{4,2}</annotation></semantics></math> is in some respects harder than computing those of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, despite the fact that the former space is of lower dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the cases where we use <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\Gamma,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\Gamma,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have to verify Pixton’s conjecture separately for the corresponding <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In genus 0 and 1, the cohomology is fully known leaving nothing to check.
For <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,6})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,6})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{5}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,4})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{5}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,4})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the intersection matrix over a finite field was computed.
The lower bound obtained from Pixton’s relations in this way was then compared against the corresponding known Betti number.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Bookkeeping challenges</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implementing a simple version of the program was relatively straightforward.
The vast majority of the work went into refining it to a level where it can actually compute something that was not already known.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the group actions we consider, whenever possible we want to work with a single representative of each orbit, rather than doing computations on the whole orbit and then taking a weighted sum.
This then results in a convention on how ordering relates to the group action:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider for example the action by <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g,n}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g,n}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If we have a representative of an orbit, that gives us enough information to reconstruct the orbit provided we know which two marked points are being permuted by the
<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-action.
We are only going to precompute a basis for <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> once, namely with the convention that the first two points are the ones being acted upon.
Thus, whenever we permute the marked points, for example when applying the permutation induced by <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> from Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Deligne’s pushforward weight spectral sequence ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we need to make sure that this convention is being respected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Besides the action of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>, there are the actions by <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> and its subgroups <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore there is the action of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on the collection of graphs.
The bookkeeping required to make sure all the corresponding ordering conventions are respected and interact well with each other turned out to be one of the main challenges of the implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> could already compute <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{g,n}^{r}/P^{r}_{g,n})^{\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the majority of <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> is agnostic with respect to the <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> action.
Similarly while the data structure of a graph in <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> naturally comes with an ordering of edges, as a whole <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> is agnostic with respect to this ordering.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Computations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using our implementation we computed the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> in the cases <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,N)\in\{(1,6),(2,4),(3,3),(4,1),(5,0)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,N)\in\{(1,6),(2,4),(3,3),(4,1),(5,0)\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We used a computer with a <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>-core <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math>-thread cpu and half a terabyte of memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to compute the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, we used Deligne’s pushforward weight spectral sequence for <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math> and Deligne’s pullback weight spectral sequence for <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first computed bases for all the required <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{r}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here in many cases we only computed a basis for invariants under some group action as described in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Computing the tautological ring of the moduli space of stable curves ‣ 5. The computer implementation ‣ Deligne’s weight spectral sequence and tautological cohomology of the moduli space of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
Each case could be computed within two weeks and the largest cases used almost all of the memory.
The rest of the computation was split into many smaller tasks.
The dependency graph for these tasks is not a tree.
We wrote a simple parallel programming task scheduler to deal with this.
It also reschedules failed tasks (due to memory overload) and dynamically adjusts the number of allowed tasks when restarting the ones that failed.
This is needed because the tasks vary a lot in terms of how much memory they require.
Furthermore, running tasks in their own processes guarantees that the memory gets released afterwards, which is relevant because <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">admcycles</span> has memory leaks.
Running all these tasks took roughly 2 weeks.
Computing the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> took a similar amount of time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After computing those of <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, computing the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{ct}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{rt}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{rt}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> required relatively limited extra computation time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In principle our program can compute all the weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> (as usual, assuming that Pixton’s relations are all relations), but in practice we cannot due to memory constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Further applications</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we will describe some cases for which one could apply (a generalization of) our program to compute weight graded pieces of the cohomology of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Remark 3.7]</cite>, for all <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3g+2n&lt;25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3g+2n&lt;25</annotation></semantics></math> we can restrict to the tautological contributions to the <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page in Deligne’s pullback weight spectral sequence to get the correct <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-page. This extends the range <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is conjectured in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to be the optimal range (this conjecture is also proven for <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+n\leq 150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+n\leq 150</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming that Pixton’s relations are all relations, we could also restrict to any single choices of <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n,p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n,p,q</annotation></semantics></math>, such that all cohomology groups appearing in <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{-p+i,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{-p+i,q}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}_{1}^{p+i,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}_{1}^{p+i,q}</annotation></semantics></math>), for <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, are tautological. This holds for instance for <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{E}_{1}^{p+i,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{E}_{1}^{p+i,q}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n,p</annotation></semantics></math> and even <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, and also for <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=6</annotation></semantics></math> when assuming that <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite>. See further in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Theorem 8]</cite> for some other cases with <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> even and <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Presently, the program only works with tautological cohomology, but it would in principle be generalizable if one knows how the non-tautological classes behave under gluing maps. An example would be <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is non-zero only for <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>), and for the non-zero classes the pullback under gluing maps is described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Section 3]</cite>. Using this description a possible extension of our program would compute the weight <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> graded piece of the compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> (since all odd contributions up to weight <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> to the cohomology of <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\tilde{g},\tilde{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\tilde{g},\tilde{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g},\tilde{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g},\tilde{n}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
